  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 310 AM EDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\n- Areas of Fog Tuesday Morning\n\nWith a surface high building into the region later today into\ntonight, winds will gradually tail off after dark and likely go\ncalm for many areas. GFS MOS guidance along with NAM soundings do\nsupport areas of fog developing after 06z Tuesday as dew point\ndepressions approach or reach zero in some spots and an inversion\nabove the surface develops. Scattered clouds could be present,\nhowever, so a widespread fog event does not appear likely. Still,\nthere is enough of a signal to put areas of fog in the forecast.\nFog may develop especially in low lying areas, which could have\nsome localized impacts to the Tuesday morning commute.\n\n- Thunderstorms Possible Wednesday, Less Likely Thursday\n\nMidweek rain chances remain on track as upper troughing is set to\nmove through the Great Lakes during peak heating on Wednesday.\nLow level moisture advection ahead of this trough is shown by\nmodel guidance, with surface dew points starting the day (12z) in\nthe upper 50s, then climbing to the mid to perhaps upper 60s by\nlate afternoon. SBCAPE values shown by the RRFS are forecast to\nreach 1500 J/kg. Some model guidance supports up to 2000 J/kg,\nthough this would likely be the higher end of what materializes.\nWhile deep layer shear looks to be about 25 kts, thus leading to\nquasi-organized convection, one supporting factor to some locally\nstronger storms is RRFS modeled DCAPE values of 800-1000 J/kg.\nWe're expecting at least some scattered showers/storms to develop\nprimarily after 18z and lasting to about 06z. Better coverage may\nget going after 21z. A scattered damaging wind gust is not out of\nthe question Wednesday afternoon and evening, so will monitor\ntrends.\n\nThe GEM and ECMWF support a slight chance for a few afternoon\nshowers and thunderstorms Thursday mainly east of US 131 as \nresidual surface based instability could remain underneath upper \ntroughing. The GFS dries out the low levels quicker and would \nlargely end the threat. The NBM POPs are quite low on Thursday, so\nrain is not a high threat at this point.\n\n- Active Pattern Could Develop By This Weekend\n\nBoth ensemble and deterministic model guidance is signaling a\npotential shift in the synoptic pattern by this weekend into early\nnext week. In fact, both the 00z ECE and CMC ensemble mean 500 mb\nheights are in the upper 580s dm across Lower MI by Sunday. This\ncould potentially place the Great Lakes in stronger mid level flow\nwith mid level jet cores moving overhead. The positioning of the\nupper ridge will be key to the location of convective activity. At\nthis point we may be in an active corridor or close to it. PWAT\nvalues of 1.50\"-2.00\"+ are supported by ensemble guidance. So,\nthese are some interesting trends to keep watching. We may round\nout the month of August and enter September with some periods of\nstorminess, especially Sunday and beyond.",
